Collected: 2026-11-18
Licence: other — EPTA DR2 (Zenodo 8300645), CC BY 4.0; Ding et al. 2023 table transcribed as data,
  cited.
Sources:
- J. Antoniadis et al. (EPTA), The second data release from the European Pulsar Timing Array I. The
  dataset and timing analysis, A&A 678 (2023) A48; data on Zenodo, record 8300645
  <https://zenodo.org/records/8300645>
- H. Ding et al., The MSPSRpi catalogue: VLBA astrometry of 18 millisecond pulsars, MNRAS 519 (2023)
  4982-5007, Table 5 (arXiv:2212.06351) <https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.06351>
Evidence class (rule 10): check
Where the Earth's radius enters:
- PSR_ROWS[].a, PSR_AU: the timing model places each observatory by its terrestrial coordinates (the
  daily delay of up to 21 ms) and takes the Earth's orbit shape from DE440, whose scale rests partly
  on radar ranging
What would vindicate the flat model (rule 5): Timing latitudes that disagree with the
  interferometric ones by more than their errors allow, the same way for every pulsar (no single
  astronomical unit), or pulses showing no annual delay at all.
